+# Decision: Concurrency Reconciliation Policy
+
+## Context
+
+Workers submit visit edits from tablets that can be offline for up to 48 hours, and sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes in the field. Two edits can therefore arrive against the same starting state without either worker knowing about the other. The record store must turn whatever arrives into a single current record without discarding either submission, and without letting arrival order stand in for scientific authority.
+
+A visit's fields are not independent in a way the server can verify: some edits are unrelated (an observer name, a missing species), but others are scientifically coupled (a species identification and its cover estimate). Any reconciliation policy either has to combine fields on the server's own judgment, or route the whole decision to a coordinator.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+Weighted criteria:
+
+- **Review workload on the two-coordinator model** (major) — tenet: *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator.* At roughly 6,000 visits/season, how much coordinator attention a policy demands is a real operating cost, not a one-time implementation concern.
+- **Risk of silently crossing a scientific relationship between fields** (major) — tenet: *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*, and *Do not silently discard a submitted edit.* A policy that combines fields it cannot verify are independent can produce an exported record neither worker actually submitted, with no review step that would have caught it.
+- **Implementation and ongoing maintenance burden** (moderate) — rules for combining scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries have to be written, kept correct as the visit schema evolves, and trusted.
+- **Fraction of visits reaching export readiness without coordinator action** (moderate) — directly trades against review workload; a policy that resolves more automatically gets more visits into Friday's export on their own.
+- **Field-worker legibility of the resolved outcome** (minor) — whether a worker who submitted an edit can recognize what became of it. A fully-merged record can contain pieces a worker never reviewed and might not recognize as "theirs."
+
+No gate eliminates an option here: both candidates satisfy the inherited constraint that no submitted edit is ever discarded.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+Each edit names the revision it started from. The server accepts it as the new current revision only if that base is still current; otherwise it becomes a competing revision and the visit enters review. A coordinator resolves a contest by choosing one whole revision — never a synthesis of several — to become current. Example: a visit at revision R2 receives two submissions both based on R2; both become competing revisions (R3a, R3b) and the visit is contested until a coordinator picks one.
+
+- Review workload: **weak fit.** Every pair of concurrent edits creates review work, including edits to fields that are obviously unrelated (an observer-name fix next to a missing-species addition).
+- Scientific-relationship risk: **strong fit.** The server never combines fields; a coordinator always sees and chooses between whole submitted revisions, so a coupled pair of fields can never be split across an automatic merge.
+- Implementation/maintenance burden: **strong fit.** No per-field combination rules to write or keep correct as the schema evolves.
+- Export readiness: **weak fit.** Any concurrent pair, however unrelated, holds the visit out of export until resolved.
+- Field-worker legibility: **strong fit.** The record a worker submitted either becomes current intact or is visibly superseded intact — never partially present in the result.
+
+Summary: trades review volume for a reconciliation step that can never misattribute or wrongly combine a worker's observations.
+
+### Field-Aware Three-Way Merge
+
+Each edit supplies its base state and its changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically into a new current revision; overlapping changes on the same field go to review. Example: one worker fixes an observer name while another adds a missing species against the same base — both land in the merged revision with no coordinator involved; two workers who both revise the same species' cover estimate go to review.
+
+- Review workload: **strong fit.** Only genuinely overlapping edits reach a coordinator; the common case of unrelated concurrent edits resolves itself.
+- Scientific-relationship risk: **weak fit.** The server cannot tell a scientifically coupled pair of fields (a species ID and its cover estimate) from an unrelated pair by field name alone, so it can combine edits that change the visit's scientific meaning in a way neither worker reviewed.
+- Implementation/maintenance burden: **weak fit.** Requires combination rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, each needing its own correctness case and upkeep as the visit schema changes.
+- Export readiness: **strong fit.** More visits reach a mergeable state without coordinator action, since only true overlaps are held back.
+- Field-worker legibility: **partial fit.** A worker's submission can end up only partly reflected in the merged record, combined with another worker's changes they never saw.
+
+Summary: trades a maintained, field-name-driven notion of "unrelated" for a materially smaller review queue.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Chosen: Whole-Visit Revisions.**
+
+This goes against the review-workload and export-readiness criteria, where field-aware merge is the stronger fit — a real cost, given the season's volume and the two-coordinator model. It wins on the major criterion the project weighs more heavily here: the risk that a server-side merge rule combines fields across a scientific relationship it cannot see, producing an exported value neither worker actually submitted. That risk sits on the *Keep the path ... inspectable* and *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* tenets, and no amount of field-combination-rule refinement removes it — the server cannot learn the full set of scientifically coupled field pairs from field names. Whole-visit revisions also win field-worker legibility, a secondary but non-trivial benefit: a worker's submission is always either fully current or fully (and visibly) superseded.
+
+**What this forecloses:** the server will never combine two submissions' fields on its own judgment. If the review queue proves unsustainable at season volume, the fix is operational (more review bandwidth, or narrowing what counts as a conflicting base) rather than a quiet reach for automatic merging — reopening that question means revisiting this decision, not patching around it.
+
+**What this obligates downstream:**
+- **Tablet-sync** must track the base revision ID of the state a worker is editing, submit it with every edit, and give a worker a way to understand that their edit is now a competing revision rather than the visible current state — including the case where they keep editing from a current revision while an unrelated contest on the same visit is still open.
+- **Review-export** must provide a revision-comparison view for resolving a contest (showing each competing revision as a whole, since the coordinator is choosing between them, not line-editing a diff) and must exclude any visit with an open contest from the Friday export set, regardless of which fields the competing revisions actually touch.
+
+**Why field-aware merge stays worth reconsidering:** it is not eliminated by a gate — it is a live alternative that wins on exactly the criteria the field data will determine the real cost of. If, once the system is running, most concurrent edits turn out to touch disjoint fields and the review queue becomes the dominant coordinator cost, revisiting this decision with real volume data is reasonable. What would have to be true to flip the choice: either a trustworthy, bounded way to declare which fields are scientifically coupled (so merge rules could safely treat the rest as independent), or evidence that the inspectability risk is smaller in practice than the review-workload cost in practice. Neither is true today, which is why this decision does not adopt it now.
