+
+Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work: a visit retrieval response reports the visit's current revision (the last coordinator-resolved or uncontested revision) and, separately, whether a contest is open against it.
+
+## Concurrency Policy: Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+Each visit is a sequence of revisions. Every revision carries a server-assigned revision ID, the author, the server receipt time, the full visit payload, and the ID of the base revision the worker started editing from (null for a visit's first revision).
+
+A submission is accepted as the visit's new current revision when its declared base revision equals the visit's current revision at the moment the submission is received. A submission whose declared base revision does not equal the current revision is stored as a **competing revision**: it does not change the current revision, and it opens or joins the visit's contest.
+
+**Contested state.** A visit is contested while it holds one or more competing revisions awaiting resolution. While contested, retrieval continues to return the last agreed current revision (so field workers and coordinators are never shown nothing) alongside an indicator that a contest is open, per REC-005. A contested visit's current revision is not treated as reviewed — review-export's eligibility rules (defined in that node) will need to account for this.
+
+**Resolution.** A coordinator resolves a contest by choosing exactly one of its competing revisions to become the visit's new current revision. The resolution outcome records the coordinator's identity, the chosen revision ID, and the IDs of every competing revision considered, per REC-006. Revisions not chosen are retained as superseded, never discarded, per REC-004. A worker who wants to combine ideas from both resubmits a further revision based on the newly current one — the server never does this combination itself.
+
+This preserves every submitted visit as the worker produced it and needs no merge rules, at the cost of review work for edits to unrelated fields as readily as for genuinely conflicting ones — the trade-off is recorded below.
 
